-- A QUALIFIED spelling inside a refinement predicate -- `List.length(_)`
-- rather than the bare `len(_)` -- used to parse, typecheck, and enforce
-- NOTHING (2026-07-30 through 2026-08-02): predicates were never run through
-- the expression desugarer, so `List.length` stayed an `EField` chain rather
-- than the dotted `EVar` the measure alias keys on, and the obligation this
-- contract looked like it generated was silently skipped.
--
-- As of 2026-08-03, `Desugar.desugar_ty` flattens a module-path call head
-- found inside a `TyRefine` predicate the same way an ordinary call head is
-- flattened -- a narrow slice, not the general expression desugarer -- so
-- `List.length(_)` here means exactly what `len(_)` means. `inner([1])`
-- below now genuinely satisfies the precondition (length 1 > 0) rather than
-- merely compiling past a skipped obligation.
--
-- This stays an `accept/` witness on purpose: the exit code was always the
-- point, and it is unchanged (0) either way -- `--check` cannot distinguish
-- "skipped" from "proved" from its exit status alone. What DID change is
-- pinned in `test/test_refinecheck.ml`'s `qualified-predicate` suite: no
-- warning fires for this program now (the alias is live), and a companion
-- case proves the precondition is genuinely enforced (an empty-list call is
-- rejected as a violation, not silently accepted).
--
-- The bare spelling that always enforced is already witnessed by t115-t118,
-- t128 and t130.
--
-- See specs/lang/refinement-types.md, "A qualified spelling in a predicate
-- now works (narrow desugar slice)".
mod T136 do

  fn inner(xs : {List(Int) | List.length(_) > 0}) : Int do
    List.length(xs)
  end

  fn main() : Int do
    inner([1])
  end

end
